Formatting and renderer regression tests
=======================================

On a modern development host, run from bsd/top:
    tests/run.sh

The suite includes the real screen.c against a fake tty/termcap terminal;
it checks visible cells, attributes, bounded allocation and restoration.
Two hundred deterministic randomized frames compare the visible terminal
with the requested contents. Focused checks cover unchanged frames with no
output or flush, changed spans, prompts, resize extremes and startup failures.
It extracts fixed(), header_text() and the process-row block from top.c,
and includes the actual format.h. No copied implementation is substituted.

The typed helper tests exhaust all 65,536 signed16 values on the host and
check padding, string precision and buffer guards. The row test compares
128 full rows with the former printf layout. The fixed-point test contains
88 literal golden results for rounding, carries, zero/exact values and
32-bit boundaries. The header test changes time, users, load values, query
success/failure and width while checking that each call still samples.

For an optional larger arithmetic check on a host with Python 3 and cc:
    python3 tests/fixed-oracle.py
It compares the actual fixed() with an independent 64-bit oracle across
274,630 power-of-two residue, word-range and 32-bit boundary cases. This
supplements the native tests; host long arithmetic can be wider than 32 bits.

On native 2.11BSD, run from bsd/top:
    /bin/sh tests/native-format.sh

That runner uses simple sed extraction and native cc -O -i in a newly made
private /tmp directory. It checks 23 signed16 boundaries, all precision and
buffer cases, the 128 process rows, and all 88 fixed-point golden cases.
It does not change tty settings or read kernel memory. Initialized test
arrays are static because the native compiler rejects automatic aggregate
initialization. Native printf ignores a negative dynamic width; the test
oracle spells left alignment as an explicit '-' flag with a positive width,
which preserves the intended semantics on both native and modern libc.
Each group prints a success message; the final marker is:
    all native top formatting tests passed

The smaller /bin/sh tests/native-fixed.sh remains available for only the
88 numeric goldens. Both native runners keep an explicit failure status
until all checks pass: this old shell can reset $? before an EXIT trap.
Cleanup removes only each runner's own temporary files. Native
execution is required to check the target's 16-bit int, 32-bit long and libc.

The bounded live-process workload is documented in README.workload.
The performance observer and repeatable commands are documented in bench/.
